WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.958
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.750
SIMON MARGOLIS: My
name is Simon Margolis.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.140
I direct the Cloud Platform
practice at SADA Systems,

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.660
and SADA's had the pleasure
of working with our panelists

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.362
to help them with
their Cloud journey.

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:18.070
I want to go through
panel here, and just

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.861
give a quick introduction,
and ask everyone

00:00:19.861 --> 00:00:22.870
to maybe introduce yourself and
talk a little bit about what

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.100
you do.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.030
Without further ado, Anastasia
Zamyshlyaeva with Reltio,

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:29.830
please.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.538
ANASTASIA ZAMYSHLYAEVA:
Thank you, Simon.

00:00:31.538 --> 00:00:35.530
My name is Anastasia,
and over seven years ago

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:39.460
I co-founded a company
which is named Reltio.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.160
Our goal was to
build products that

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.950
allow us to organize all
of the enterprise data

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.500
in one single place.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.600
Let me give you an example.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.880
Let's say I'm working in a
wealth management company,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.050
and there is not that
many opportunities,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.290
and finding new clients
is extremely hard.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.510
Let's say in my system I have
a person whose name is Scott,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.640
and he's a software engineer,
and most likely I'll

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.680
be not targeting this person.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.010
But from other sources
from the enterprise,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.370
I realize that there are
more details about Scott.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:20.030
Scott is working in company
XYZ, and recently in the news,

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.040
we learned that it had a very
successful round of funding.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.990
Based on all the statistics
through the enterprise,

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.880
we know that these kind of
companies are going IPO.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.470
There are more details.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.700
Scott actually was the first
developer in this company.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.280
Now you see that
all this context

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.300
is giving a completely
different flavor to Scott.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.360
Now as a person working in
a wealth management company,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.420
I need to reach out to this
person as fast as possible,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.740
because two years from now,
it will be already too late.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.220
But the problem is
that in the enterprise

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.830
there is no single place
where I could get all

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.960
of this information together.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.090
That's why at Reltio
our mission was

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.870
to build a place where I can
have reliable data from all

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.180
over the enterprise
that I can transform

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.860
into relevant insights.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.800
I also want to have
recommendation frameworks,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.600
so instead of scanning through
millions of profiles of Scotts,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.530
I can have just an
alert in the system.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.590
Hey, Stasia, you need to
work with this person.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.900
If you think about
the enterprise,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.510
it was an example about
wealth management.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:36.520
But in every enterprise, there
are the same kind of problems.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.530
You want to leverage
all the information

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.470
about individuals,
organizations, products,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.974
to drive your business.

00:02:44.974 --> 00:02:46.800
SIMON MARGOLIS:
Thank you, Stasia.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.277
Next, Aaron Brown with MadHive.

00:02:49.277 --> 00:02:51.360
If you could, tell us a
little bit about yourself.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.800
AARON BROWN: Hi,
my name is Aaron.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.890
I work at MadHive.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.630
I'm the VP of engineering.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.610
MadHive is a New York
based ad tech firm.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.140
For those who
don't know, ad tech

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.050
is the science
and infrastructure

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.940
behind advertising.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.790
It comes with a lot of
engineering challenges.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.110
We deal with very,
very large scale.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.160
There are real-time
exchanges for ads.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.226
Advertisers don't
know where you're

00:03:16.226 --> 00:03:17.600
going to go on
the internet next,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.830
and you just appear,
and we have to make sure

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.587
that the right
advertisement gets there.

00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:25.170
There are a whole lot more ads
on the internet than just about

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.530
anything else, so it gets big.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.990
The data science is
also very important.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.900
If we show you the wrong
ad, then you just pass off,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.130
and that's money lost.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.370
The other thing we work on
very heavily is privacy.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.890
That's almost sort of
contradictory in that industry,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.620
but privacy is very,
very important.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.879
We believe privacy is a human
right, first and foremost.

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:54.420
Advertising is kind of creepy
in that regard-- sort of follow

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.336
you around everywhere.

00:03:55.336 --> 00:03:57.930
You looked at that bicycle
on Amazon one time,

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.080
and it's everywhere you go.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.554
That's sort of weird, and
even in an innocent case,

00:04:04.554 --> 00:04:05.220
a little creepy.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.750
The nefarious cases
are very creepy,

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.080
so we'd like to stop that.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.330
Also, advertising has problems
because of that huge scale,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.910
and because of all those very,
very low latency requirements,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.500
there are tons and
tons of companies

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.410
involved in the
advertising supply chain--

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.550
maybe sometimes 20 companies
just to show you one ad.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:31.230
When you have 20 companies
working on data in an industry

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.870
where data is your
value, you want

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.610
to work together
and check notes,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.960
but you don't want to
show each other anything.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.590
What happens is there
are very, very large

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.760
discrepancies, sometimes
to the order of 10%,

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.980
which is not great because ads
are kind of like digital money.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.080
We're like the original
digital currency.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.810
Each one of those little
ads is some money.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.840
When you're being fast and
loose with people's money,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.440
it's a big problem.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.980
We try to solve all these
things at once with privacy.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.260
Privacy is about securing
data to make sure

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.680
that it's not tampered with--
always in your control.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.770
This works really
well for us consumers,

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.530
and also works really, really
well for us in the ad tech

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:25.040
business, because now when data
has provenance and control,

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.420
the stuff like doing
accounting, auditing,

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.000
gets really, really easy.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.000
SIMON MARGOLIS: Awesome.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.550
Thank you, Aaron.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.310
Josh Gray, principal architect
with Citrix, if you could,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.810
tell us a little
bit about yourself.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:37.684
JOSH GRAY: All right.

00:05:37.684 --> 00:05:38.644
Thanks, Simon.

00:05:38.644 --> 00:05:40.560
I actually just joined
Citrix a few months ago

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.950
when they acquired my last
company, called Cedexis.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.430
What we did at Cedexis is,
for about the last nine years,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:52.080
we built the largest community
of global internet performance

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.040
data in the world.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.090
We started about 2009,
and grew it to a place

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.060
where we see about a
billion users every day.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.250
We collect tens of
billions of data points,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.330
and we aggregate that all to
give insight into what's really

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.980
going on in the internet.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.680
We effectively measure
every major public provider

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.620
of infrastructure, many private
providers of infrastructure.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.345
We watch whether
there's congestion,

00:06:17.345 --> 00:06:19.470
how the throughput is
behaving, what the round trip

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:20.820
times look like.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.150
We roll all that data
through a very rich analytics

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.070
and reporting
infrastructure, and we also

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.190
do a very active, very
low latency global traffic

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.290
management based on that.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.470
There's a lot of very high
skill, very high speed,

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:37.770
very low latency use
cases that we deal with.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.740
The nature of the
business that we're in

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.880
is that we are
actually consumers

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.200
of virtually all of these
providers that we monitor.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.790
We keep an eye on
them, but we also

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:52.600
run a 24/7, 365, global
service on top of them.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:57.510
And Google has been our
fastest growing vendor

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.540
that we use as it's continued
to capture use cases

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.340
in our own infrastructure.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.280
We continue to be
multi-cloud, multi-vendor,

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.920
and that will likely
continue, but on the merits

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:10.260
of what we see with the
use cases that we capture,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.260
it's been a really
exciting journey

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.560
to keep moving things forward.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.560
SIMON MARGOLIS: Awesome.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.040
Thank you, Josh.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.740
Then, last, we'll go to Glen
Pringle, innovation lead

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.820
at Monash University.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.670
GLEN PRINGLE: Thanks, Simon.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:29.310
Monash University is the
largest university in Australia.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.930
Although we're
only 60 years old,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.590
we've got presence
across four continents,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:39.060
and we're the number
one university

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.080
for engineering and technology
in Australia, as well.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.580
Being such a large
university, we've

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.010
got a really diverse
set of needs.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.160
We have numerous researchers.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.000
We're a research-intensive
university.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.960
They have enormous
different needs.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.260
We have educators.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:58.980
We have a very large
number of students.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.410
We've actually found
that we are using Cloud

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.450
more and more so
that they can meet

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.880
a lot of those diverse needs.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.270
So as an innovation lead, I'm
working with those people,

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.330
and looking to really help
them to make use of Cloud,

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.726
and in particular Google
Cloud, to achieve that.

00:08:15.726 --> 00:08:17.340
SIMON MARGOLIS: Thank you, Glen.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.710
I'll actually start
with you, since you're

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.320
holding the microphone.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.140
I wanted to ask you a little
bit about what really drove you

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.970
to start adopting the Cloud.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.511
Then, if you could,
talk a little bit

00:08:28.511 --> 00:08:31.700
about why Google Cloud in
particular stood out to you.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.960
GLEN PRINGLE: Sure.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:37.799
We didn't actually approach
Cloud looking to reduce cost.

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:39.480
We were looking for
a number of things.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.870
We were really looking
for flexibility.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:46.750
As we're trying to achieve those
things I mentioned earlier,

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:50.520
we're wanting solutions where
we can quickly and easily

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.030
be able to use the resources--

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.740
and looking for a real
range of different resources

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.530
to do a lot of different things.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:02.340
Speed of delivery is a
really big thing, too.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.440
Previously, if we were
working with infrastructure,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.950
it took a lot of
time to prepare,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.780
to get that
infrastructure ready,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.899
to bring it into production.

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:13.440
Whereas, when we're
going with Cloud,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.830
it's available when we need it.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:21.450
It's not just being able
to bring it in instantly,

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.700
it's also about
being able to use

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.550
that really wide range
of different resources,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:29.750
and bring them in
as we need them.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.610
Scalability-- as I
already mentioned,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.640
being able to ramp up
with infrastructure.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.830
When we used to
run into problems,

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.980
we would have to go
and order new hardware.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.430
If we wanted to scale up, there
would be up to months' time

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.500
before that became available.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.180
Whereas, once
we're in the Cloud,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:54.150
we now see that using auto
scaling, that hardware, that

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.110
compute is available before
we even know that we need it.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:04.610
As we look at the issues
around people, process,

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:08.410
and technology, we've actually
found that using Cloud

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.920
takes technology out of that.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.580
It becomes the simplest
part of those three.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.980
We're no longer
really struggling

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:19.139
to deal with that technology
side like we used to be.

00:10:19.139 --> 00:10:20.430
SIMON MARGOLIS: That's awesome.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.210
That's great to know.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.270
So actually my next question,
right next to you, is for Josh.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.469
JOSH GRAY: We didn't
plan this, really.

00:10:27.469 --> 00:10:29.260
SIMON MARGOLIS: This
is only starting well.

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.830
We'll have to play hot
microphone in a little bit.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.260
So you mentioned you've worked
in a few different Cloud

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:35.920
providers.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.684
You work in a few different
environments overall.

00:10:38.684 --> 00:10:40.350
What challenges are
you looking to solve

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:43.390
that lead you towards
looking for some alternatives

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:45.190
to what you already
had in your wheelhouse

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:46.825
and eventually to Google?

00:10:46.825 --> 00:10:47.450
JOSH GRAY: Yes.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:52.370
Our early use case that
brought us to Google Cloud

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.260
is we have really, really,
really large data sets.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.780
Tens of billions of
rows with hundreds

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:03.490
of columns and attributes
that are collected constantly.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:07.450
We have a lot of really low
latency cases to get insight.

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:10.180
Our data gets a lot less
interesting the more

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:11.680
time passes.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.836
As days, weeks, hours
go by, if that's

00:11:14.836 --> 00:11:16.210
how long it takes
to get results,

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:20.230
sometimes that's not
meaningful anymore.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:24.970
Our main use case was to extract
more valuable information out

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:30.370
of our data sets and make it
more accessible to our teams.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.070
We ended up looking through
a lot of different data

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:36.340
architectures, and have tried
virtually everything that

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.850
exists, and got really
attracted to BigQuery early on,

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:41.050
and pushed it hard.

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:44.050
We had many millions
of tables of data,

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.180
did some good work
with the BQ team

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:49.090
on refining our approaches
so that it aligned

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.010
with their expectations,
and ended up

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:54.250
getting some really,
really amazing results.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:57.850
To me, there's a lot
of solutions out there.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.070
There's a lot of options
for data and analytics

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:05.117
and that kind of approach.

00:12:05.117 --> 00:12:06.700
But what really made
the difference is

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.370
it was truly a managed service.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.100
You just dumped data
into it, and then you

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.380
have SQL access to it.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.620
You don't have to think about
things like instant sizing.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:21.610
There's truly a managed
layer to it that just works.

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:25.450
The fact that I could do
multi-terabyte inquiries

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:27.850
with SQL, which the
vast majority of my team

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.610
was already familiar
with, really

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:33.130
made it so that people started
pulling out value a lot

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:33.730
more easily.

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.940
There was no training, no
specialized familiarity

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:38.800
of a new framework,
or a new programming

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.700
model that had to be done.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:45.560
That got us into BigQuery.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.250
It has been a large part
of our infrastructure

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:49.480
for several years now.

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.730
We continue to
grow and push that.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.730
We've actually been
layering in other use cases.

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:58.780
We do a lot of things just with
instances in global management.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.880
We do a lot of edge computing.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.890
The quality of engineering
resulting-- we're

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.810
in the business of
watching all these Cloud

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:08.182
providers and all these CDNs
and all these data centers.

00:13:08.182 --> 00:13:09.640
We get a tremendous
amount of data,

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.510
not just through
our own collection,

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:15.370
but also with our customers
who help us collect this data.

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.050
Just on the merits
of a lot of these,

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:20.090
like the engineering of the
edge platform between the Clouds

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:22.660
is just really impressive work.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:26.740
A lot of the capabilities
for very rapid changing

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.260
of virtualized resources--

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.530
again, really, really,
really good results.

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:33.727
As a result, we've
had increasing tiers

00:13:33.727 --> 00:13:35.560
through our stack that
have gotten picked up

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:39.222
with the solutions that are
running on Google Cloud.

00:13:39.222 --> 00:13:40.430
SIMON MARGOLIS: That's great.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:43.990
It sounds like there were
some unique things to GCP that

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.480
allowed you to do things you
otherwise would have, I guess,

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.120
had a lot of pain around.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:49.920
JOSH GRAY: It was
absolutely true.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.440
There's one other dimension,
too, A lot of the offerings,

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:57.010
they were externalizations
of very mature technology--

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.660
BigQuery's based on
the Dremel paper,

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:01.074
which is 10-ish years old.

00:14:01.074 --> 00:14:03.490
Not only had there been a lot
of good thinking, good work,

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.980
but the solution had
a lot of miles on it

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.870
with their own internal usage.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:12.040
We've found that, frequently,
even the newer offerings,

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.340
they're new to us on the
outside, but a lot of them

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:17.830
have been in use
for five, 10 years,

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.290
and they have a lot
of experience baked

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.660
into both the implementation
and the design that

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:25.450
really makes for a really,
really solid offering.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:26.450
SIMON MARGOLIS: Awesome.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:27.790
Thank you for that.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:30.420
The next question actually
is for you, Stasia.

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:32.510
It's a relatively
similar question,

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:35.740
which is we know you
shifted over to use

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:37.210
GCP for some of your workloads.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:40.750
Can you explain why you chose
GCP, and what kind of workloads

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:42.392
you're using it for?

00:14:42.392 --> 00:14:43.600
ANASTASIA ZAMYSHLYAEVA: Sure.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.280
There were actually
different stages

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:50.440
for us moving to Google Cloud
with different mutations.

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.990
Let me cover some of
the use cases maybe

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.780
you can correlate to yours.

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:59.450
The first case, we were
running our platform in Amazon,

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:04.034
and we had a lot of APIs,
users were using UIs,

00:15:04.034 --> 00:15:05.450
and we wanted to
understand what's

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:09.290
the usage of the platform,
what customers are doing,

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:11.300
what's the cost for
different customers.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.640
So we collected all
of this information

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.110
in one single place.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:17.090
It was Google BigQuery,
where we could

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:20.300
store all of this information
at very cheap cost,

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.360
and then have a querying
mechanism on top of that.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.660
We could build the reports that
we could use internally, as

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:28.970
well as share with customers.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:30.980
Then there is also a
very nice addition.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:35.480
Google has a product
called Google Data Studio

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.970
that allows us to visualize--

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:39.410
it's kind of a BI tool.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:42.530
It allows us to visualize
for us the reports,

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.020
and, again, share with
customers and ourselves,

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.120
understanding what is
actually happening.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:48.770
This is the first use case.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:52.910
It was long before when you
migrated the whole platform.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:57.680
Another use case was we wanted
to build product from scratch.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:00.260
We decided to go with
a relatively small team

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:05.780
of developers and deliver this
product as fast as possible

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:08.060
without any data ops.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:11.390
For this we used managed
services from Google.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.270
We used App Engine
with all the caches,

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:18.650
with indexes that are
there, with data store.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:23.000
We were able to deliver
this product very fast.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.960
One very nice thing, when you
roll out this kind of product,

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:31.610
is the cost grows as you grow.

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:34.970
Initially, if you don't
have usage of that product,

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:37.640
you're actually not
paying for this much.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.230
When you have the
adoption, this is

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:41.510
when you start paying,
as well as the auto

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:43.310
scaling that is still there.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:47.199
Right now, if you don't have
usage at the current moment,

00:16:47.199 --> 00:16:47.990
you are not paying.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.570
But when there are
a spike of requests,

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:54.590
this is when we can easily
scale up and meet the customer's

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.540
requirements.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.660
Right now, I think the
biggest one is actually--

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:05.630
when we moved our platform
from Amazon to Google--

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:07.609
the reason for this,
the motivation here,

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:13.550
was we wanted to meet the
requirements for our customers

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.250
who wanted to be in
Google, and didn't want

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.040
to be in other Cloud providers.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:21.859
With this, it was one
of the motivation.

00:17:21.859 --> 00:17:25.400
Other motivations that
we heard is, frequently,

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:29.150
people who are migrating
to Google get better cost

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:30.650
efficiency.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:34.340
And the reason for this
is because of the network

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:37.300
that you have with
Google, the pricing model,

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.370
the flexibility-- how we can
switch between the instance

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:41.570
types.

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:43.930
So it was one of
the motivations.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:48.290
Another one was we wanted to mix
and match the best technologies

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:51.500
from different Cloud providers,
not tying to a specific Cloud

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:52.640
provider.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:57.350
If there is a database that can
give replication across regions

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.840
and very fast response
time, we would

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.670
like to wrap it
into our product,

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.950
and give it to our customers.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:05.870
Another example is
machine learning.

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:09.270
We want to take all of this and,
again, give to our customers,

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:13.790
no matter if they're residing
in one Cloud or another.

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:19.014
That's three use cases why
we're switching to Google Cloud.

00:18:19.014 --> 00:18:20.680
SIMON MARGOLIS: Those
are great stories,

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.580
and, obviously, it's been great
to be a part of that journey

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:24.380
and see those
transformations that you've

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.990
been able to recognize, both
from a technology perspective,

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:30.860
as well as from a
business perspective.

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:34.400
Actually, Aaron, I have a
similar question for you,

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:35.894
which is--

00:18:35.894 --> 00:18:37.310
I think I was
really lucky to work

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:40.400
with you during the assessment
process for figuring out

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.780
where you wanted to
run your new workloads.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:46.715
You have some really
interesting requirements.

00:18:46.715 --> 00:18:48.830
So I was wondering if you could
talk a little bit about what

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:51.620
those nuanced requirements are,
and what the decision making

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:57.650
process was like when you were
looking for a Cloud provider.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:01.710
AARON BROWN: Well, a lot
of the team at MadHive

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:05.294
we've plucked from other
very smart start-ups.

00:19:05.294 --> 00:19:06.710
So we built a very
impressive team

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:09.659
who have experience
across all Clouds.

00:19:09.659 --> 00:19:11.450
When you get the best
of the best together,

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:15.610
they say, I want to work
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:16.420
It's a funny story.

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:18.980
We actually tried to
hire Simon to do that,

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:23.966
and then we found out that we
don't have to pay for SADA.

00:19:23.966 --> 00:19:25.340
We get SADA for
free with Google.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:26.500
It's great.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.150
The best of the best.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.880
Things that were
super important to us

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.250
for rolling out
our products were

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:37.850
first and foremost serverless
managed everything,

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:41.150
and we mean that to
the absolute extreme.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:45.621
Because when you have
these elite engineers,

00:19:45.621 --> 00:19:47.120
what you don't want
them to be doing

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:50.120
is sitting there restarting
EC2 Instance all day.

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:51.740
You want to be paying
them to do what

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.020
they are absolutely amazing
at and just creating

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.210
stuff all day.

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:58.020
They talk about Google
Cloud as cheaper,

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:01.050
or like gigabytes and
gigahertz, and this and that.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.100
Yes, that's true.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:04.675
But that's thousands of dollars.

00:20:04.675 --> 00:20:06.300
You know, when you
talk about salaries,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.531
that's millions of dollars
saved, plus opportunities

00:20:09.531 --> 00:20:10.030
captured.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:16.420
It lets us really out-maneuver
lots of companies in the space,

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:18.590
and it's gotten us
very quickly here

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:21.570
on the stage talking what
we do, which is cool.

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:22.580
Thanks, Google Cloud.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:30.390
A couple of things that
SADA helped us with

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:32.430
was in advertising.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:36.420
Sometimes the workloads can
get really, really spiky--

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.160
like really spiky.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:41.550
All of North America is waiting
for 8:00 PM for the show

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:42.690
to start.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.030
They just wait, and
then at that one second,

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:49.200
there's this tsunami of
requests that comes at you.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:52.470
There is no product on
any Cloud that scales

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:53.790
like App Engine's standard.

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:56.520
You can just put
it and forget it,

00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:03.120
and that is absolutely critical
to us, because ads are money.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.710
There's literal money.

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:07.842
If you drop one request,
that's lost money.

00:21:07.842 --> 00:21:10.050
SIMON MARGOLIS: I remember
working with you early on.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.870
We identified an area where I
think we saved-- what was it?

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:14.490
Maybe 100 milliseconds
of latency,

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:16.777
and you were so excited
about that because

00:21:16.777 --> 00:21:18.360
of all the additional
things you could

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:21.900
do with those
additional milliseconds.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.330
I love that story
because it really

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:26.250
speaks to what you
can do when you're

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:29.550
working at the scale
of what GCP provides.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:31.994
AARON BROWN: Yeah, you can do
a lot of database look-ups,

00:21:31.994 --> 00:21:32.910
machine learning work.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.170
You can do a lot of stuff
in 100 milliseconds.

00:21:37.664 --> 00:21:39.330
SIMON MARGOLIS: As
long as we're talking

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.788
about large scale, Josh, I
think I have a question for you,

00:21:41.788 --> 00:21:42.920
as well.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:44.920
You kind of hinted at
this in your introduction.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:48.140
You process just a
tremendous amount of data,

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:51.060
and you need to get
insights from that data.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:54.000
Can you tell us a little bit
about what, within GCP, has

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.060
helped you overcome the natural
hurdles that come from having

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:00.040
to achieve goals like yours?

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.280
JOSH GRAY: Scale
is always something

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:06.370
that we design for and
constantly are evolving

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.480
and work on significantly.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:12.930
I mean our business is up
many orders of magnitude

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:14.850
per every few years.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.610
Now that we are part of a
bigger family with Citrix,

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:21.120
we're optimistic that will keep
moving in the same direction,

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:22.405
probably at a faster pace.

00:22:22.405 --> 00:22:24.030
I'm going to leave
right after the talk

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:27.250
to go get some work done.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:29.570
But the main tools with scale--

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:32.180
I'll go back to,
again, the agility.

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:37.410
A couple of things that stand
out is the experience of GCP.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:42.180
When you look at the APIs,
they're just generally

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:45.990
remarkably well done, and easy
to understand and consistent.

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:48.750
That leads to velocity
with how quickly

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:51.480
people can write systems and
interact with them that take

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:53.880
advantage of the capabilities.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:56.010
That is not always
a true experience

00:22:56.010 --> 00:23:03.150
across different providers,
where the inconsistency ends up

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:07.830
burning time as you figure
things out and get them dialed.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:11.490
The services themselves--
velocity and reactivity,

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:14.790
you can feel, whether it's
App Engine's ability to scale,

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.950
or the load balancer's
ability to scale,

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:20.490
or the ability to hot
migrate instances.

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:23.250
There's just this
underlying design ethos

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:27.270
that your platform is at least
as fast as you want to go,

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:28.800
and that's always
good, because you

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.260
know you're the limiting
factor in your design.

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.630
You're not you're not
banking on infrastructure

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.240
that is going to
put a limit on it

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:37.620
that you may not
even know about.

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:39.690
It may not even be
in the documentation.

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.540
That's always a really
important aspect.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:46.770
That, in particular, is what
has pushed some of our use cases

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:50.640
beyond even BigQuery out
into moving on to running

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:52.920
on Google's Platform.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.760
How they approached edge--

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:56.650
we're all about speed.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:59.520
We run out of about 100
different points on the planet,

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:02.190
because we do, among other
things, global DNS services,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:06.000
and that's where 10 or
20 milliseconds is a lot.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.646
You don't want to take that.

00:24:07.646 --> 00:24:10.020
You don't want to spend that
in transit time for somebody

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.150
to be able to get
an answer for you.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:17.370
And being able to take advantage
of the breadth of the data

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:18.960
centers, and the
data center growth,

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:21.930
and the commitment to expansion,
and in the architecture

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:22.870
of that edge--

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:26.970
where they any cast external
IPs and have some capabilities

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:32.220
to bring your resources for
fulfillment closer to where

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:33.640
the requests originate.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:35.640
Those are all really,
really important features,

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:40.170
and things that have earned
the respect of our team

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:42.780
as they explore and
develop and grow

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:44.424
on those kinds of capabilities.

00:24:44.424 --> 00:24:45.840
SIMON MARGOLIS: I
think it's worth

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:48.423
mentioning-- you mentioned the
acquisition, but prior to that,

00:24:48.423 --> 00:24:51.000
you were really doing a lot of
this with a very small team.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.550
I imagine there's a
lot of what GCP offers

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:55.290
that facilitated that, right?

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:58.170
JOSH GRAY: Yeah, we
had 20-some engineers,

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:03.450
and we deliver many billions
of mission critical decisions,

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:04.890
and collect many
tens of billions

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:08.160
of data points every day
for companies like LinkedIn

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:10.446
and Pinterest and Airbnb.

00:25:10.446 --> 00:25:12.570
We're running as part of
the plumbing that delivers

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:14.850
all of those sites to you.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:18.120
Again, we do that with
20-some engineers.

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:21.400
A lot of that's about
choosing very carefully.

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:25.190
I talked about the
efficiency of the APIs,

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.060
and the efficiency
of the tooling,

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:29.950
and taking advantage of
the right-managed tiers

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:31.424
for the right services.

00:25:31.424 --> 00:25:33.090
You're not always
running raw instances.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:35.040
We do some of that for
our highly custom stuff,

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.373
but you're also taking advantage
of things like BigQuery

00:25:37.373 --> 00:25:38.640
for manage--

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.800
I don't have to worry
about managing a data pool,

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.140
and I don't have to worry
about deciding on my sharding

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:43.770
and scaling.

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:47.880
I just dump data in, and
put SQL against it, and out

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:49.860
of the chute with no more
investment than that,

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.930
I get pretty darned
good results.

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.000
I can actually even
then grow from there

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.900
to get into some more
specialized use cases.

00:25:56.900 --> 00:26:01.200
It's like Aaron mentioned,
too, that you want your people

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:03.480
spending their time on
the most important part

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:04.290
of your solution.

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:06.600
You don't want them spending
their time on the parts

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:10.740
that somebody else, frankly,
can do better than you.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.900
The implicit part of
that I want to talk about

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:15.540
is you're also, by
extension, taking

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:19.020
those people who run the managed
services as part of your team.

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:23.130
If they mess up their service,
that results in your service

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:26.430
not being of the quality
that you want to do.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.766
Which products you
choose, and the quality

00:26:28.766 --> 00:26:31.140
of the product they've built,
and the quality of the team

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:34.200
that runs it, is important.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:37.140
Across a variety of providers
and a variety of products,

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:40.470
I think we've all seen that that
isn't always a super high bar.

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:44.040
So choosing carefully
is an important part

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:47.010
to maintaining team
velocity and scaling,

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.030
so that 20 people
can run something

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:53.070
that runs mission critical
services for so many companies

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.960
around the globe.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.470
SIMON MARGOLIS: That's
a great perspective.

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.560
Continuing this theme
of massive scale, Glen,

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:04.200
I would like to turn
to you, as well.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:06.750
I think we've had the unique
privilege of working together

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:09.540
on two distinct projects,
a massive G Suite

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:15.030
project, and then a similarly
massive GCP implementation.

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.990
Being that Monash is one of the
largest universities in terms

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:21.690
of G Suite users, certainly the
largest university in Australia

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:24.396
in terms of students,
can you tell me

00:27:24.396 --> 00:27:25.770
a little bit about
what synergies

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:28.840
you found between
G Suite and GCP,

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:32.640
especially on the scale
you're operating on?

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:37.500
GLEN PRINGLE: We initially
went with G Suite back in 2009.

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:41.040
At that point in time, we
were one of the largest

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.610
organizations-- well, definitely
the largest university

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:46.360
in Australia going
with G Suite--

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:49.410
one of the largest in the world.

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:53.080
We rolled that out over a couple
of years, and since that time,

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:57.840
we've seen a constant growth
in the use of G Suite.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:01.320
Right now, we've got
about 120,000 active users

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:03.990
within G Suite,
and we've continued

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:07.500
to see a lot of increase in
the use of the various apps.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:14.920
As we have pretty much every
active student and staff

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.640
member using it, it's a
very natural progression

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:22.300
for a lot of those users who
want to do a little bit more

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.175
to be able to move
in to using GCP.

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:31.840
There is no effort for them
to actually begin using GCP.

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:35.290
With other systems, they often
have to go and get access

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:36.040
given to them.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:39.220
They need to be given
to the resources.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:43.540
Whereas because they're
Google users through G Suite,

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:47.140
they can then just
begin using GCP.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:49.870
It's been interesting watching
over the last couple of years,

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:52.840
especially, how a
lot of our students

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:56.590
are beginning to try a
lot of different things.

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.820
They're developing projects
on their own, in some cases.

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:03.730
Our researchers can
very easily get access

00:29:03.730 --> 00:29:05.980
to resources that
otherwise have to go

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:08.200
through all sorts
of other mechanisms

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.640
to get those resources.

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:12.970
Just the fact that
they're Google users

00:29:12.970 --> 00:29:15.640
every single day makes
it really, really

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.590
easy to make that transition.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:20.005
SIMON MARGOLIS: That's great.

00:29:20.005 --> 00:29:22.130
The things you were able
to talk about where you're

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:26.440
able to enable your students
to do really impressive work,

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:30.620
I think is a great story that
is, I think, unique to Monash.

00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:32.080
That's very cool to see.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:36.250
Stasia, I think, back to you.

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:36.750
See?

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:40.160
I didn't plan this
all that well.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:43.377
We, again, got to work together
on your migration to GCP.

00:29:43.377 --> 00:29:45.460
While I'd love to stand
up here and say everything

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:48.040
went fantastically and
we hit no roadblocks,

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:51.210
certainly that's not the case,
as you find in large scale

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:52.600
migration projects.

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:54.141
Could you tell us
a little about what

00:29:54.141 --> 00:29:56.360
hurdles you ran into
during your shift to GCP,

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:57.940
and what you did
to overcome them?

00:29:59.765 --> 00:30:01.390
ANASTASIA ZAMYSHLYAEVA:
Actually, there

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:05.320
were not that many problems
during the migration.

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:09.790
We had really great support from
our Google team, and from SADA,

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:13.240
with a lot of experience
doing these kinds of projects.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.160
Actually, there were
not that many things

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:18.010
that we had challenges with.

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:22.470
Also, the thing that we were
migrating from AWS to GCP,

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:25.900
the architecture itself it
was designed multi-cloud.

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:29.890
It wasn't relying on any of the
managed services from Amazon,

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:34.670
and that's why it was just
rewriting some of the Terraform

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:37.570
scripts, and running the
same kind of workload,

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:42.490
but on Google Cloud.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:45.430
The whole project from
the beginning, when

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:47.140
we were making the design--

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.520
how it will be
working in Google--

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:51.190
through the testing,
as well as data

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:55.300
migration for some of the
customers, took two months.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:56.830
After two months,
we already were

00:30:56.830 --> 00:31:00.070
having the first customer
who was leveraging

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:02.530
our platform in Google Cloud.

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:05.110
I would say that,
if at that point,

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:09.160
we were using Kubernetes,
then this whole migration

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:13.220
could take much
shorter time frame.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.470
There were some lessons
learned, though,

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:17.980
especially on the pricing side.

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:21.880
Something that you
need to think about--

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.710
typically, a Serisys
would be breaking down

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:24.650
into two categories.

00:31:24.650 --> 00:31:28.600
The first one is stateless,
another stateful.

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:31.690
So with a stateless, it is
critical to use the oldest

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.300
scaling.

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:36.190
Certainly, you can go
with the alternative

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:40.790
with using App Engine, but we
wanted to have more control,

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:43.840
and have faster migration
to Google Cloud.

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:46.680
In this case, think
of your scalability.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:52.420
If you want to have the cost
efficiency for the baseline

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:58.120
capacity, its better to go
with committed use notes,

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.590
and then you will have
the discount for those.

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:02.080
But for the older
scale part, which

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:07.140
is typically 80% of your
cluster, go with preemptibles.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:09.910
The benefits of this-- you
will have the bigger discount,

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:13.060
you will have better cost model.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.520
But those notes could
be taken at any time.

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:18.940
But for older scale,
it should be fine.

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:22.000
For the stateful, if
you have an ability

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:25.450
to switch to managed service,
this will be the best.

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:27.970
First of all, the
older scale is there

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:31.240
that, typically, you will
not get from a database

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:35.380
that you are managing, as
well as the cost, because

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:39.670
of the multi-tenancy that
Google already takes care of.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.360
If you have to
manage your database,

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:43.960
then go and buy
all the instances

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:46.920
as committed use notes,
because typically databases

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:48.640
are not scaling down.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.560
These are the main
lessons that we

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:53.826
learned that were
different from Amazon.

00:32:53.826 --> 00:32:54.950
SIMON MARGOLIS: Absolutely.

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:56.324
It seems to be a
recurring theme,

00:32:56.324 --> 00:32:58.960
where there is this need
amongst many of our customers

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:00.330
to look at--

00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:02.410
I mean, as we all see
from this conference,

00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:04.300
there's a lot of
stuff that Google's

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:06.640
doing-- some really, really
cool, powerful things that

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:08.774
are sort of new to the market.

00:33:08.774 --> 00:33:10.690
This is something we see
regularly is the need

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:12.430
to look at all of
these offerings

00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:16.474
and figure out what are the
best tools for your use cases.

00:33:16.474 --> 00:33:17.890
Actually, Aaron,
the next question

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:20.140
would be for you on
that same thread,

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:22.210
because you're using
a lot of App Engine,

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:23.590
you're using a lot of Bigtable.

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:26.830
These are things that are
somewhat unique to Google.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:28.330
Can you talk a
little bit about what

00:33:28.330 --> 00:33:30.220
makes those advantages
for your business

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:32.560
versus your competition?

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.840
AARON BROWN: Yeah, that's easy.

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:40.810
These are the things that
scale absolutely infinitely.

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:45.100
Bigtable was my first look into
the potential here for Google

00:33:45.100 --> 00:33:47.380
as a platform for advertising.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:50.050
When we were just us getting our
feet wet, running some queries,

00:33:50.050 --> 00:33:54.970
and getting results back
as fast as the network

00:33:54.970 --> 00:33:59.230
card on my machine in
their office could go,

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:01.900
I knew we were on to something.

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:04.660
Since then, it's only been
more and more and more--

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.060
out of the box, it's phenomenal.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:09.750
When you add on more
layers, you know

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:11.980
that that great
engineering team has

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:14.500
made it do incredible magic.

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:19.000
I can't imagine doing
advertising at the scale

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:23.110
that MadHive does running
a bunch of sharded MySQL

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:25.780
and engine acts,
and stuff like that.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:28.800
It just wouldn't happen.

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:30.219
Surprise!

00:34:30.219 --> 00:34:33.670
Google knows a thing
about advertising.

00:34:33.670 --> 00:34:41.929
SADA has helped us stay very
Google-y inside of MadHive.

00:34:41.929 --> 00:34:43.929
It's a great resource for us.

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:45.345
SIMON MARGOLIS:
Like you said, you

00:34:45.345 --> 00:34:47.670
have a small team of
really bright people.

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.987
You don't waste their
time managing the stuff

00:34:49.987 --> 00:34:51.070
they don't have to manage.

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:53.278
AARON BROWN: No, they have
enough work to do already.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:59.620
SIMON MARGOLIS: Actually, Glen,
my next question is for you.

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:01.740
Monash University has
a lot going on, right?

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.936
It's a massive university.

00:35:03.936 --> 00:35:05.560
Can you tell us a
little bit about what

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:07.143
you're seeing, in
terms of the ability

00:35:07.143 --> 00:35:10.859
to better innovate across
the campus by leveraging ECP?

00:35:10.859 --> 00:35:11.650
GLEN PRINGLE: Sure.

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:17.470
As a couple of people
already mentioned,

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:22.240
Google App Engine provides
a lot of opportunity.

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:28.060
We almost exclusively
use that aspect of GCP,

00:35:28.060 --> 00:35:30.940
and it provides
us the opportunity

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:38.380
to both cheaply and easily put
into place customized solutions

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:40.910
to different problems.

00:35:40.910 --> 00:35:43.060
As a couple of examples
of the sorts of things

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:47.980
that we're seeing, we recently
had the campus security group

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:49.190
approach us.

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:51.370
They had an issue
that they wanted

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:55.450
to be able to try to track
the shuttle buses that

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:57.700
run around campus.

00:35:57.700 --> 00:35:59.440
In the lead-up to
the exam period,

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.500
they wanted the students
to be able to find

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:04.510
when that bus was
going to arrive,

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:07.510
so that they could
know where to head for,

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:11.360
and when to get there
to get to their exam.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:14.620
The previous solution that
they had had fallen apart.

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:17.770
We had almost no time
to develop this in.

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:20.860
Within five days
we were able to--

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:24.190
with one dev, one engineer--

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:26.230
put together a solution.

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:30.790
We used IoT devices
on those buses,

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:33.340
and from there we
imported the data.

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:39.940
We used Google App Engine,
Firebase, and also Google Maps.

00:36:39.940 --> 00:36:42.580
We had a solution
up, from very start

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:45.790
of the request coming in
to the working solution,

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:48.190
within five days.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:50.650
That's the sort of thing
that is just about impossible

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:55.510
to achieve using other methods.

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:58.080
In terms of quick and
easy, pulling together

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:03.360
the different components, that
worked really well for us.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.920
Now, another other problem--

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:08.430
and this is a different scale.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:10.950
The Institute of
Railway Technology

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:15.240
is a group within
our university who

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:17.790
deal with a lot of
issues on railroads,

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:21.150
and they have a lot of
monitoring analytics

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:23.700
and those sorts of things.

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:26.520
They work with
mining trains that

00:37:26.520 --> 00:37:27.930
run in the Outback
of Australia--

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:32.442
some of the harshest conditions
you can get on the planet.

00:37:32.442 --> 00:37:33.900
One of the interesting
things, they

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:36.300
put tracking equipment
on those trains.

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:40.500
I was talking to Simon
about this the other night.

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:43.930
If something goes wrong
with these devices,

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:47.100
it can be up to two years
before they can get access

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:48.580
to be able to replace them.

00:37:48.580 --> 00:37:51.540
So when you talk about downtime
if something goes wrong,

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.810
it's rare that you get more
than a couple of years downtime.

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:58.770
They've got to be sure that
everything works really well.

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:01.440
These IoT devices are
tracking a whole range

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:02.360
of different things.

00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:08.130
They're tracking things like
vibrations, sound, location,

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:10.020
all sorts of different things.

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:12.240
The idea is that they want
to be able to understand

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:17.370
where sections of track
actually need maintenance.

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:20.430
That data is being fed
back to Google, where

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:22.206
we can do analytics on it.

00:38:22.206 --> 00:38:24.080
There's a whole range
of different components

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:29.220
that we can pull together
from GCP to actually do that.

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:31.440
One of the most exciting
areas that I'm actually

00:38:31.440 --> 00:38:34.890
finding with that is in the
whole ML space and things

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:38.460
like autoML Vision
and other things that

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:40.680
are providing enormous
opportunities to actually

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:43.147
enhance what we can
do in this area.

00:38:43.147 --> 00:38:45.480
SIMON MARGOLIS: Yes, I think
we're hearing a theme here,

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:48.210
where you're able to do a
whole lot more with a whole lot

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:51.420
less, in terms of human
resources, in terms of time

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:56.020
to deployment, even in terms
of scale in the case by RT.

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:58.530
It's really interesting.

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:00.390
On that same vein,
Josh, I know when

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:01.980
we started working
together, you were

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:05.700
dealing with a relatively
speaking much smaller deal

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:07.637
of data than you are today.

00:39:07.637 --> 00:39:09.720
Can you talk a little bit
about what, if anything,

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:11.730
really had to change
in order to accommodate

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:14.130
the significant increase in
volume that you encounter

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.185
today?

00:39:15.185 --> 00:39:15.810
JOSH GRAY: Yes.

00:39:18.350 --> 00:39:22.000
We've been on a
classic startup path.

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:24.220
You start with one,
and you're super

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:27.220
excited about your first
customer that brings you

00:39:27.220 --> 00:39:28.270
a little bit of traffic.

00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:31.900
Then you just you grow
and grow and grow.

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:34.330
Now we're part of
the Citrix family,

00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:38.020
and Citrix is all
about choice and Cloud

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:43.150
and optimizing and mobilizing
delivery of data and apps.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:46.210
We're really excited about
playing a significant role

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:46.870
in that.

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:55.740
Our architecture and our product
has evolved several times

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:59.080
as we've gotten
larger, and as we've

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:02.290
taken on more and more vendors.

00:40:02.290 --> 00:40:06.180
On day one, we operated--

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:08.350
I think we might have had
a little bit of Amazon,

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:11.500
but we did a lot of
lease Dyer and CoLOS

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:14.320
and leased
infrastructure from CDNs,

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:19.780
and there was a lot of
interesting early architecture.

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:21.280
A lot of the ways
that we've evolved

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:23.590
that to deal with
scale, again, was

00:40:23.590 --> 00:40:29.860
to continue to leverage the
fact that our system is very

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:32.170
layered, and has a
lot of microservices,

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:34.570
and has a lot of
internal decomposition

00:40:34.570 --> 00:40:37.060
so that we can move
those things forward.

00:40:37.060 --> 00:40:39.610
The nice side
effect of that is we

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:42.580
can move entire tiers of
our internal operations

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:45.970
in how they operate, what
technologies they use,

00:40:45.970 --> 00:40:51.740
what vendors they use, and we do
that all while running a 24/7,

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:55.030
365, zero downtime operation.

00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:56.740
We don't get to say,
I'm sorry, we're not

00:40:56.740 --> 00:40:59.110
going to resolve DNS names
for the next two hours,

00:40:59.110 --> 00:41:01.900
because we need to do
some work on the system.

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:04.660
Any given second of
every single day,

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:07.310
we will get hundreds of
thousands of requests,

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:08.980
sometimes many more.

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:11.230
As Aaron was talking about,
a lot of our customers

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:13.410
deal with news and media
and things like that,

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:15.490
so sometimes you
will get spikes.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:17.740
We do a lot of work
with game delivery.

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.560
Christmas morning is a
remarkable time for us,

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:23.800
as well, because everybody is
opening up their game consoles

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:28.090
and downloading updates
to all of their games.

00:41:28.090 --> 00:41:31.564
You get this incredibly spiky,
sometimes very unpredictable--

00:41:31.564 --> 00:41:32.980
I mean, you can
predict it's going

00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:35.105
to happen on Christmas
morning, but you never quite

00:41:35.105 --> 00:41:37.720
know how large it's going to
be, or exactly when it'll hit,

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:40.120
or things like that.

00:41:40.120 --> 00:41:43.270
So you need to keep leveraging
that the layers in your system

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:48.250
can be as reactive and adaptive
and resilient and everything,

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:50.170
and that's been a
key as we've evolved.

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:52.704
It's a big reason why
we've taken on the layers

00:41:52.704 --> 00:41:55.120
that we've taken on-- that
we've moved into Google Cloud--

00:41:55.120 --> 00:41:59.770
because they delivered
uniquely reactive, uniquely

00:41:59.770 --> 00:42:05.590
resilient capabilities
that we leverage.

00:42:05.590 --> 00:42:10.120
Sometimes it really
matters in the details.

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:12.309
A lot of people talk about
auto this or auto that,

00:42:12.309 --> 00:42:14.350
but when you actually peel
the onion a little bit

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:17.410
and do some testing,
well, it's sort of auto,

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:20.110
and it's sort of
fast, and it might

00:42:20.110 --> 00:42:21.790
take a few minutes
or a few hours,

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:24.670
or you might hit a hard limit on
the resources in a data center.

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:26.253
Those things are all
really important,

00:42:26.253 --> 00:42:28.630
because at the end of the
day, our customers don't care.

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:31.630
We either deliver the
service or we don't.

00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:33.670
The latter is really
not an option for us,

00:42:33.670 --> 00:42:35.890
so we choose our vendors wisely.

00:42:35.890 --> 00:42:37.690
We choose our
technologies wisely,

00:42:37.690 --> 00:42:40.990
and we've been continuing
to evolve that.

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:44.200
The one last point is being
part of the Citrix family

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:46.780
has been really exciting,
because we're leveraging

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:49.480
a lot of our best practices
across some of the other Cloud

00:42:49.480 --> 00:42:53.110
offerings, and desktop
delivery offerings, and Cloud

00:42:53.110 --> 00:42:55.810
utilization scenarios.

00:42:55.810 --> 00:42:57.460
We're looking
forward to bringing

00:42:57.460 --> 00:43:00.370
a lot of that same kind
of mentality and approach

00:43:00.370 --> 00:43:02.950
to the broader offerings
and the good work

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:06.185
that they're already doing
in a lot of those spaces.

00:43:06.185 --> 00:43:07.810
SIMON MARGOLIS: It's
an awesome insight

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:09.393
that you're able to
provide, because I

00:43:09.393 --> 00:43:13.279
think your team generally has
stayed pretty similar in size.

00:43:13.279 --> 00:43:15.070
Echoing what the other
panelists have said,

00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:17.200
you've really been
able to do so much

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:20.920
more by leveraging
various components of what

00:43:20.920 --> 00:43:22.570
Cloud has to offer.

00:43:22.570 --> 00:43:25.450
I think it's a great story.

00:43:25.450 --> 00:43:27.400
So, Aaron, I have
another one for you,

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:30.850
just because this is one of my
favorite things to talk about.

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:33.910
We joke all the time that
you deal with Blockchain,

00:43:33.910 --> 00:43:36.550
and you deal with
Cloud computing.

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:39.250
Are there any other
buzzwords that I'm missing?

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:39.916
AARON BROWN: AI.

00:43:42.762 --> 00:43:44.470
SIMON MARGOLIS: These
sound like I'm just

00:43:44.470 --> 00:43:46.780
trying to sell a white
paper or something by saying

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:49.071
blockchaining and Cloud
computing in the same sentence,

00:43:49.071 --> 00:43:50.740
but it's what
you're really doing.

00:43:50.740 --> 00:43:53.470
Can you tell us a little bit
about what those things do

00:43:53.470 --> 00:43:55.900
together, and how you're
able to achieve what you

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:57.529
do using those technologies?

00:43:57.529 --> 00:43:59.320
AARON BROWN: This is
a really good question

00:43:59.320 --> 00:44:01.810
that we get a lot, because
it's not a secret that we're

00:44:01.810 --> 00:44:04.960
very big users of Google Cloud.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:09.400
We're also very active in
the Blockchain community.

00:44:09.400 --> 00:44:11.860
Our researchers dedicate
tons of time to it.

00:44:11.860 --> 00:44:14.450
We have a lot of contributions
that we're super proud of.

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:17.570
But people who are familiar
with Blockchain are like, hey,

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:21.520
this is literally
a Cloud competitor.

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:25.480
The Cloud is all about
centralization and authority,

00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:27.460
and Blockchain is all
about decentralization,

00:44:27.460 --> 00:44:29.200
and not having to trust--

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:32.770
knowing things are true
through cryptography.

00:44:32.770 --> 00:44:34.960
They actually work really,
really well together.

00:44:37.737 --> 00:44:39.820
So if you recall, we talked
about in the beginning

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:43.300
about one of the big problems
in advertising is auditing.

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:49.480
Ads are literally money.

00:44:49.480 --> 00:44:50.920
The auditing
standards should even

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:53.770
be higher because
this is a case where,

00:44:53.770 --> 00:44:56.420
unlike banking on Wall
Street where there

00:44:56.420 --> 00:44:59.060
are shares and dollars
and things that go around,

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:00.550
ads get made.

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:02.754
You just create money.

00:45:02.754 --> 00:45:04.420
If you have opportunity
to create money,

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:08.380
and you have a lot of
opacity in the supply chain,

00:45:08.380 --> 00:45:11.320
there's going to be problems.

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:15.120
One of the things
that we do is--

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:17.710
I'm going to walk back
through this whole process.

00:45:17.710 --> 00:45:19.840
You can use cryptography.

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:24.160
Cryptography is more than
just encrypting messages.

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:27.100
Cryptography is
about also validating

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:30.790
the integrity of data, without
necessarily revealing why.

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:35.110
For example, if we
have REST Endpoints,

00:45:35.110 --> 00:45:39.500
most REST Endpoints are
like, there's a web request,

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:46.270
it does a database query, and
then it gives back a result.

00:45:46.270 --> 00:45:48.290
With the addition of
cryptography on this,

00:45:48.290 --> 00:45:52.330
we can make it that the data--
even though you didn't reveal

00:45:52.330 --> 00:45:55.570
what happened underneath--
we can reveal the provenance

00:45:55.570 --> 00:45:58.270
and the supply
chain of that data.

00:45:58.270 --> 00:46:00.490
Then that's super
useful, because now it's

00:46:00.490 --> 00:46:03.860
just as easy as a REST
Endpoint, but now I get to know,

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:06.070
OK, this data has some validity.

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:08.530
It went through partners, and
it went through transforms,

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:11.500
and I don't have to know
exactly what those transforms

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:13.860
were to know that it's correct.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:16.210
Because if even
one thing changed,

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:18.610
everything just breaks.

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:20.780
Cryptography is neat like that.

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:27.370
The other thing we can do is
you want some universal base.

00:46:27.370 --> 00:46:30.190
We have this string,
almost, that carries

00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:34.690
through all the data as it
goes through the supply chain.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:36.820
That's where the Blockchain
part really comes in.

00:46:36.820 --> 00:46:38.470
It becomes really
useful, because that

00:46:38.470 --> 00:46:42.040
is our universal truth where
everyone in the industry

00:46:42.040 --> 00:46:44.520
is working really, really
hard to maintain this

00:46:44.520 --> 00:46:46.840
to some base level of truth.

00:46:46.840 --> 00:46:48.610
The math gets
complicated, but just

00:46:48.610 --> 00:46:50.784
say we're all agreeing
that the number is five.

00:46:50.784 --> 00:46:52.450
The number is five
right now, and that's

00:46:52.450 --> 00:46:54.940
where I'll anchor our strings.

00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:58.330
Now across the entire
ecosystem, we're

00:46:58.330 --> 00:47:02.680
doing REST calls, database
lookups, queries, transforms,

00:47:02.680 --> 00:47:06.950
behind people servers
in large data centers.

00:47:06.950 --> 00:47:09.520
But we know with absolute
cryptographic certainty

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:11.770
that the answers are correct.

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:15.790
Also, when we're done sharing
data we can revoke that.

00:47:15.790 --> 00:47:19.540
Immediately, it goes all the
way through the supply chain,

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:21.700
not by trust, not
by contracts, not

00:47:21.700 --> 00:47:26.940
even by privacy laws like
the new European regulation

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:29.110
of privacy, GDPR--

00:47:29.110 --> 00:47:31.060
literally by the
force of physics.

00:47:31.060 --> 00:47:33.010
Cryptography.

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:35.620
That's a really, really
exciting solution

00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:38.080
to be able to offer
in our industry,

00:47:38.080 --> 00:47:41.950
and we've been working with
the Blockchain standards

00:47:41.950 --> 00:47:44.680
body for advertising,
called AdLedger.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:48.490
We're also one of the founders,
along with IBM and Techna

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:51.280
and a bunch of large companies.

00:47:51.280 --> 00:47:53.500
So we're happy to make
that contribution.

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:57.790
But I think the most exciting
thing about that combination

00:47:57.790 --> 00:47:59.110
is--

00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:00.822
you talked about
Cloud and Blockchain--

00:48:00.822 --> 00:48:02.530
I've been attending
a lot of the sessions

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:05.920
here about machine learning.

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:10.390
Google and Cloud is all about
AI and machine learning.

00:48:10.390 --> 00:48:13.810
That is the killer use case.

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:16.840
But machine learning and AI
are only as good as the data

00:48:16.840 --> 00:48:18.910
that you can source.

00:48:18.910 --> 00:48:21.370
While Google has done an
amazing job of collecting up

00:48:21.370 --> 00:48:22.870
tons and tons of
data in one place--

00:48:22.870 --> 00:48:24.870
and it shows in their
products-- there

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:27.010
is a limit to that,
because we can't all

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:31.780
share our business data or, as
consumers, our private data.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:34.940
We probably don't even want that
to go into these big machine

00:48:34.940 --> 00:48:35.800
learning things.

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:40.484
That is a hard stop for
the evolution of AI.

00:48:40.484 --> 00:48:41.900
It shouldn't have
to be like that.

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:44.230
We can use the same
privacy techniques

00:48:44.230 --> 00:48:47.440
that we discussed for
consumers and across these data

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:51.970
supply chains, and you can put
that into machine learning.

00:48:51.970 --> 00:48:53.780
That's the killer app.

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:59.120
AI plus cryptography
is everything.

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:00.860
That's literally
the new internet.

00:49:00.860 --> 00:49:01.930
I think that's what
we're going to be talking

00:49:01.930 --> 00:49:03.550
about here in the next decade.

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:04.510
SIMON MARGOLIS: No,
I love that story

00:49:04.510 --> 00:49:06.640
because I think that is
the common misconception

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:09.472
is that these are somehow
competing technologies.

00:49:09.472 --> 00:49:10.930
But you really do
paint the picture

00:49:10.930 --> 00:49:14.360
as to how these things can
actually be very complimentary.

00:49:14.360 --> 00:49:18.865
I love squashing
that bad stereotype.

00:49:18.865 --> 00:49:21.240
Unfortunately, I think we're
just about out of time here.

00:49:21.240 --> 00:49:23.830
I just wanted to thank all of
you for being here with us.

00:49:23.830 --> 00:49:26.310
Thank you all for
attending, and we will all

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:28.140
be somewhere around
here afterwards

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:30.000
if anyone has any
questions for us.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.470
Thank you, everyone.

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:34.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

